<Header home="#top" install="#run" />

<main id="top">
  <!-- The first screen: one note, two readers. -->
  <section class="hero" data-state="checked" aria-labelledby="h-hero">
    <h1 id="h-hero"><span>Tell your AI agents why the number moved</span> <span>before they guess.</span></h1>

    <div class="pane person">
      <p class="who"><span class="face" aria-hidden="true">S</span>A person asks, in the browser</p>
      <div data-s="checked" id="p-checked">
        <p class="ask">How did orders in Norway change in October 2026?</p>
        <p class="state-line"><b>Checked</b> This is written down and checked.</p>
        <p class="ans"><span class="hlx">A new customs rule stopped all online sales to Norway from 4 August 2026, so orders there fell to almost nothing.</span><a class="fm" href="#note" data-to="n1" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</a></p>
      </div>
      <div data-s="conflict" id="p-conflict">
        <p class="ask">How were orders counted in the United Kingdom in October 2026?</p>
        <p class="state-line"><b>Conflict</b> Two items say different things.</p>
        <p class="ans"><span class="each">One says they count store credit.<a class="fm" href="#note" data-to="c1" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</a></span><span class="each">One says they are card and cash payments only.<a class="fm" href="#note" data-to="c2" aria-label="Footnote 2">2</a></span></p>
      </div>
      <div data-s="missing" id="p-missing">
        <p class="ask">Why did returns in Sweden jump in March 2023?</p>
        <p class="state-line"><b>Not found</b> Nothing is written about this yet.</p>
        <p class="ans">Nothing is written down for Returns in Sweden in March 2023.<small>That's not the same as there being no reason.</small></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="seam" id="note">
      <article class="card" data-s="checked" id="n-checked" data-anchor="n1">
        <p class="head"><span class="fm" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span>Checked by the owner of Sales</span></p>
        <h2>Online sales to Norway stopped on 4 August 2026</h2>
        <blockquote>"Parcels from webshops outside Norway are held at the border from 4 August 2026 until further notice."</blockquote>
        <p class="by">Sales, owned by Jon Abara and Sofia Brandt.<br /><b>Don't conclude:</b> fewer orders in Norway after 4 August do not mean fewer customers.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="pair" data-s="conflict" id="n-conflict">
        <article class="card" data-anchor="c1">
          <p class="head"><span class="fm" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span>Sales. Written by Jon Abara</span></p>
          <h2>United Kingdom orders count store credit</h2>
        </article>
        <span class="neq" role="img" aria-label="disagrees with">&ne;</span>
        <article class="card" data-anchor="c2">
          <p class="head"><span class="fm" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span>Sales. Written by Sofia Brandt</span></p>
          <h2>United Kingdom orders are card and cash payments only</h2>
        </article>
      </div>
      <article class="card none" data-s="missing" id="n-missing" data-anchor="m1">
        <h2>No footnote</h2>
        <p>Nobody has written this down yet.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="pane agent">
      <p class="who"><span class="chip" aria-hidden="true">&gt;_</span>An agent asks, over MCP</p>
      <div class="tty" data-s="checked" id="a-checked">
        <p class="q">How did orders in Norway change in October 2026?</p>
        <p class="guess"><s>This may reflect weaker marketing performance.</s></p>
        <p class="tool">called <b>get_context</b></p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"status":</span> "matched"</p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"say":</span> <span class="say">"<span class="hl">Checked.</span> 5 context items apply to Orders in Norway in October 2026. Nothing else was looked up. 1 of them applies to only part of that question."</span></p>
        <p class="kv"><a class="fm" href="#note" data-to="n1" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</a><span class="k">"title":</span> "Online sales to Norway stopped on 4 August 2026"</p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"not_to_conclude":</span> "Fewer orders in Norway after 4 August do not mean fewer customers."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tty" data-s="conflict" id="a-conflict">
        <p class="q">How were orders counted in the United Kingdom in October 2026?</p>
        <p class="tool">called <b>get_context</b></p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"status":</span> "conflict"</p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"say":</span> <span class="say">"<span class="hl">Conflict.</span> Two items say different things. 10 context items apply to Orders in United Kingdom in October 2026. Nothing else was looked up. 2 of them apply to only part of that question. Some of them disagree. 1 more context item matches but isn't shared with AI agents: it's high risk and waits for a check."</span></p>
        <p class="kv"><a class="fm" href="#note" data-to="c1" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</a><span class="k">"title":</span> "United Kingdom orders count store credit"</p>
        <p class="kv"><a class="fm" href="#note" data-to="c2" aria-label="Footnote 2">2</a><span class="k">"title":</span> "United Kingdom orders are card and cash payments only"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tty" data-s="missing" id="a-missing">
        <p class="q">Why did returns in Sweden jump in March 2023?</p>
        <p class="tool">called <b>get_context</b></p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"status":</span> "no_match"</p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"say":</span> <span class="say">"<span class="hl">Not found.</span> Nothing is written about this yet. Nothing is written down for Returns in Sweden in March 2023. Nothing else was looked up."</span></p>
        <p class="kv"><span class="k">"context":</span> []</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <svg class="wires" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
    <p class="sr-only" id="state-live" aria-live="polite"></p>

    <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="What is written decides the answer">
      <span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Try what's written:</span>
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-go="checked" aria-controls="p-checked n-checked a-checked">Checked</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-go="conflict" aria-controls="p-conflict n-conflict a-conflict">Conflict</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-go="missing" aria-controls="p-missing n-missing a-missing">Not found</button>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-foot">
      <div class="cta"><a class="btn btn-ink" href="#run">Install</a><a class="btn btn-line" href={docs('get-started')}>Read the docs</a></div>
      <p class="meta">{words.heroMeta}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The timeline on the seam: what happens before either of them reads it. -->
  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="h-split">
    <h2 id="h-split"><span>Before either of them</span> <span>reads a word of it.</span></h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="dot">Draft</span>
        <p class="l">Anyone can write a context item. It's short: what happened, where and from when, and how you know.</p>
        <p class="r">An agent can suggest one too, and it waits for the owner like anything else. A draft never reaches an agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step live">
        <span class="dot">Checked</span>
        <p class="l">It lands in the Inbox of whoever owns that area. They see whether the claims match the source, and what agents would say before and after. Then they publish it, or send it back with a note.</p>
        <p class="r">From now on <code>get_context</code> returns it with every answer it applies to, with its source.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="dot">Due for a check</span>
        <p class="l">When the check date passes, or a definition it relies on changes in Cube or dbt, the owner looks again: still true, or edit.</p>
        <p class="r">Until then the answer starts with <code>Outdated</code>, so the agent can say it may be old.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="dot">Not found, again</span>
        <p class="l">If questions about one topic keep finding nothing, the owner gets a card about it. It's how people find out what to write next.</p>
        <p class="r">We count the topic, not the words.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="docs">In the docs: <a href={docs('write-context')}>Write context</a> <a href={docs('review-a-suggestion')}>Review a suggestion</a></p>
  </section>

  <div class="sheet">
    <section class="band" id="fits" aria-labelledby="h-sits">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2 id="h-sits">It sits next to your semantic layer, not inside it.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Cube or dbt MetricFlow still defines Orders and still returns the figure. Footnote only reads the names, so that a note can say which metric and which market it's about. After the owner publishes the note, it goes out with every answer that uses it, as a footnote.</p>
        <div class="sum">
          <div class="part fig"><div class="pic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 60" preserveAspectRatio="none"><path d="M0,14 L24,9 L48,17 L72,11 L96,6 L120,15 L144,10 L150,52 L180,54 L210,53 L240,55 L270,54 L300,54"/></svg></div><b>The figure</b><p>Cube or dbt MetricFlow defines Orders and returns it. Footnote reads the names only.</p></div>
          <span class="op" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          <div class="part fn"><div class="pic" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fm">1</span></div><b>The footnote</b><p>Online sales to Norway stopped on 4 August. Someone in Sales wrote it, and the owner checked it.</p></div>
          <span class="op" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="part out"><div class="pic"><span>Orders in Norway fell to almost nothing from 4 August.<span class="fm" role="img" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</span></span></div><b>The answer</b><p>Claude Code over MCP, or Ask in the browser.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="plain">No model picks the context. Plain rules match the question's metric, market and dates against what's written. Footnote doesn't store numbers either. Your semantic layer returns the figure, and the item points at it.</p>
        <div class="real">
          <figure><div class="frame"><picture class="on-light"><source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('item-light-phone')} width="780" height="1560" /><img src={shot('item-light')} alt="A context item on the demo: Online sales to Norway stopped on 4 August 2026, with the sentence copied from the customs notice, its domain, owners, check date and source link." width="1600" height="1266" loading="lazy" /></picture><picture class="on-dark"><source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('item-dark-phone')} width="780" height="1560" /><img src={shot('item-dark')} alt="A context item on the demo: Online sales to Norway stopped on 4 August 2026, with the sentence copied from the customs notice, its domain, owners, check date and source link." width="1600" height="1266" loading="lazy" /></picture></div><h3>A context item is short</h3><p>What happened, where and from when, the sentence copied from its source, who owns it and when they last checked. That's all an agent reads.</p></figure>
          <figure><div class="frame"><picture class="on-light"><source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('observability-light-phone')} width="780" height="1560" /><img src={shot('observability-light')} alt="Observability on the demo: what agents asked this week, by topic and answer line, with the share of checked answers and the questions that found nothing. Counts only." width="1600" height="1250" loading="lazy" /></picture><picture class="on-dark"><source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('observability-dark-phone')} width="780" height="1560" /><img src={shot('observability-dark')} alt="Observability on the demo: what agents asked this week, by topic and answer line, with the share of checked answers and the questions that found nothing. Counts only." width="1600" height="1250" loading="lazy" /></picture></div><h3>Gaps come back as counts</h3><p>Observability shows what agents asked about, by topic and answer line, and which questions found nothing. It keeps counts, not the words.</p></figure>
        </div>
        <p class="docs">In the docs: <a href={docs('how-agents-use-it')}>How agents use it</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="lines" aria-labelledby="h-lines">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2 id="h-lines">Every answer starts by saying what it found.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Ask is the page for people. Agents get the same first line over MCP. There are six of these lines, and code picks which one, not the model, so Checked means the same thing every time.</p>
        <ul class="lines">
          <li><i class="g checked" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>Checked</b><span>Something applies, and its owner published it with a source and a check date.</span><em>Nothing to do.</em></li>
          <li><i class="g partial" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>Partial</b><span>Part of the question is covered, or the dates were assumed. The answer says which.</span><em>Write the rest, or tell the owner.</em></li>
          <li><i class="g missing" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>Not found</b><span>Nobody has written this down. That's not the same as there being no reason.</span><em>Write it, or tell the owner.</em></li>
          <li><i class="g conflict" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>Conflict</b><span>Two items apply and they disagree. Agents get both and say so.</span><em>An owner keeps one, narrows one, or keeps both.</em></li>
          <li><i class="g old" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>Outdated</b><span>The check date has passed, or a definition the item relies on changed.</span><em>The owner looks again: Still true, or Edit.</em></li>
          <li><i class="g failed" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>Not looked up</b><span>Ask couldn't look just now. It doesn't mean nothing is written.</span><em>Try again in a minute.</em></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="docs">In the docs: <a href={docs('answer-lines')}>The six answer lines</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="trust" aria-labelledby="h-owners">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2 id="h-owners">Agents only see what an owner has published.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Every item belongs to one area, like Sales or Paid media, and the owners of that area decide what goes out. Drafts, suggestions and comments stay inside.</p>
        <div class="claims">
          <div><h3>Drafts stay drafts</h3><p>The database role that answers agents can't read the versions table at all. That's a permission, not a setting someone can flip by accident.</p></div>
          <div><h3>A review before it goes live</h3><p>The review holds the claims up against the source. It also replays the questions agents asked against the draft, so the owner sees what will change before it does.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Nothing filled in</h3><p>When nothing is written, the answer says Not found and stops. It doesn't reach for a likely story.</p></div>
          <div><h3>What not to conclude</h3><p>An item can say what not to read into it. The Norway one says fewer orders there doesn't mean fewer customers, and the agent gets that line with the item.</p></div>
          <div><h3>When a definition changes</h3><p>If a definition an item relies on changes or disappears in Cube or dbt, the item is flagged for its owner, and answers say Outdated until they've looked.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Topics, not words</h3><p>When an agent asks something, we keep what it was about and how often. We don't keep the words. Your own chats in Ask stay in your browser.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="know"><b>One thing to know:</b> in this version, everyone who is signed in, and every agent, can read everything that's published. Per-person limits aren't built yet.</p>
        <p class="docs">In the docs: <a href={docs('domains-and-owners')}>Domains and owners</a> <a href={docs('privacy')}>Privacy</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="agents" id="agents" aria-labelledby="h-agents">
    <div class="inner">
      <div>
        <h2 id="h-agents">Six MCP tools, and no model on our side.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Your agent connects over MCP and signs in as you. Footnote doesn't run a model for agents, so the same question gets the same items until an owner publishes a change.</p>
        <ul class="tools">
          <li><code>get_context</code><span>The items that apply to a question about a metric, a market or some dates, with the answer line first.</span></li>
          <li><code>find_context</code><span>Look up what's written about a few words, using the names people actually say, like GMV.</span></li>
          <li><code>read_context</code><span>One item in full, with its source and its owner.</span></li>
          <li><code>get_related</code><span>Follow the links from an item, a metric or a domain. A link is not proof of a cause.</span></li>
          <li><code>report<i>asks first</i></code><span>Tell the owner an item looks wrong or unclear, after the person says yes.</span></li>
          <li><code>suggest_context<i>asks first</i></code><span>Send the owner a suggestion. Nothing goes live until they've checked it.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="after">Connecting takes one command, then one approval in the browser. An admin can switch any tool off for any agent. We tell agents that item text is data, not instructions, and in our test runs they ignored the instructions we planted in items.</p>
        <p class="docs">In the docs: <a href={docs('connect-an-agent')}>Connect an agent</a> <a href={docs('reference/agent-tools')}>The agent tools in detail</a></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>One line in your AGENTS.md</h3>
        <p>When you connect an agent, the prompt the app gives you adds one line to the project's instructions file: CLAUDE.md if you have one, otherwise AGENTS.md. That's all you write.</p>
        <div class="file"><p class="bar"><b>AGENTS.md</b><span>the line you add</span></p><pre><span># Footnote</span>
Before you answer a question about company data, call get_context first and use what it returns.</pre></div>
        <div class="file"><p class="bar"><b>get_context reply</b><span>sent every time</span></p><ol>
          <li>State the documented fact with its source and dates.</li>
          <li>Say "might be related" only when the dates overlap and the metric is in scope, and say the context does not prove a cause.</li>
          <li>Never say "caused by" unless the item states the effect.</li>
          <li>When an item has not_to_conclude, never conclude what it rules out.</li>
          <li>Suggest a check that would test the effect.</li>
        </ol></div>
        <p class="after">How to explain the why isn't left to that file. Footnote sends the rules with every reply, so each agent gets the same ones, and they change with the server rather than in every repository.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reads" id="connectors" aria-labelledby="h-reads">
    <h2 id="h-reads"><span>What it reads,</span> <span>and what reads it.</span></h2>
    <p class="how"><span>Some of this works today. Some of it is built and still waiting for someone to try it on a real account. Each row says which.</span></p>
    <div class="io">
      <div class="side in-side">
        <div><h3>Definitions</h3><p>Metric and dimension names from your semantic layer. Read only, and it works today.</p>
          <ul class="kit">
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-cube"/></svg><span>Cube<small><i class="s works" aria-hidden="true"></i>works today</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-dbt"/></svg><span>dbt MetricFlow<small><i class="s works" aria-hidden="true"></i>works today</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-lines"/></svg><span>A list you write yourself<small><i class="s works" aria-hidden="true"></i>works today</small></span></li>
          </ul></div>
        <div><h3>Documents</h3><p>Notes that arrive as suggestions for an owner. Built, not tried on a real account yet.</p>
          <ul class="kit">
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-markdown"/></svg><span>Markdown in Git<small><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>{words.markdownStatus}</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-drive"/></svg><span>Google Drive<small><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>built, not tried on a real account</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-notion"/></svg><span>Notion<small><i class="s later" aria-hidden="true"></i>designed, not built</small></span></li>
          </ul></div>
      </div>
      <span class="hub" aria-hidden="true"><svg><use href="#fn-logo"/></svg></span>
      <div class="side out-side">
        <div><h3>Agents</h3><p>Anything that speaks MCP, signed in as the person. We've tried Claude Code.</p>
          <ul class="kit">
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-claude"/></svg><span>Claude Code<small><i class="s works" aria-hidden="true"></i>tried</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-cursor"/></svg><span>Cursor<small><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>install link, not tried</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-vscode"/></svg><span>VS Code<small><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>install link, not tried</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-openai"/></svg><span>ChatGPT and Codex<small><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>written steps, not tried</small></span></li>
            <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-mcp"/></svg><span>Any other MCP client<small><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>over HTTP, not tried</small></span></li>
          </ul></div>
        <div><h3>People</h3><p>Ask, in the browser, for anyone who is signed in.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="key">
      <p><span><i class="s works" aria-hidden="true"></i>works today</span><span><i class="s built" aria-hidden="true"></i>built or linked, not tried</span><span><i class="s later" aria-hidden="true"></i>designed, not built</span></p>
      <p>In the docs: <a href={docs('connectors')}>Connectors</a> <a href={docs('connectors/authoring')}>Build a connector</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sheet">
    <section class="band run" id="run" aria-labelledby="h-run">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2 id="h-run">Try it on your own computer.</h2>
        <p class="lede">{words.installLede}</p>
        <ol class="go">
          <li><h3>Start it</h3><p>{words.startLine}</p><p class="cmd">{words.startCommands.map((command, i) => <Fragment>{i > 0 && <br />}<span>$ </span>{command}</Fragment>)}</p></li>
          <li><h3>Open it</h3><p>Sign in as one of the demo people if it asks, open Ask, and try the Norway question.</p><p class="cmd"><span>$ </span>open http://localhost:8080</p></li>
          <li><h3>Connect Claude Code</h3><p>Add the server, run /mcp, pick it and choose Authenticate. Your browser opens. Pick a demo person and press Allow.</p><p class="cmd"><span>$ </span>claude mcp add --transport http footnote http://localhost:3000/mcp</p></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="facts">
          <div><h3>Runs on</h3><p>One computer with Docker. Everything it keeps is in PostgreSQL.</p><ul class="kit"><li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-docker"/></svg>Docker</li><li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-postgres"/></svg>PostgreSQL</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>A model, if you want one</h3><p>For Ask's sentences and the answer tests. It's off unless you start Footnote with <code>MODELS=live</code> and a model key, and agents never need it. A real model costs money and receives the context Ask reads.</p>
            <ul class="kit rows">
              <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-anthropic"/></svg><span>Anthropic<small>the default: Footnote is built and tested on Claude</small></span></li>
              <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-openai"/></svg><span>OpenAI</span></li>
              <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#l-vercel"/></svg><span>Vercel AI Gateway</span></li>
              <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-server"/></svg><span>Any server that speaks either API<small>such as OpenRouter, LiteLLM, or Ollama on your own computer</small></span></li>
            </ul>
            <p class="fine">Other models than Claude work, and their answers are less complete. <a href={docs('model-providers')}>Choose a model provider</a></p></div>
          <div><h3>Your tool</h3><p>A connector is one folder in the repository. The guide is written so a coding agent can follow it.</p></div>
        </div>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li>On the demo nothing leaves your computer, and no AI service is called. Ask uses a scripted reader that lists what's written.</li>
          <li>Footnote reads your definitions from a Cube model folder or from the semantic_manifest.json that dbt parse writes, and never writes back.</li>
          <li>{words.versionNote}</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="end"><a class="btn btn-ink" href={docs('self-hosting')}>Install</a><a class="btn btn-line" href={docs('get-started')}>Read the docs</a><span class="meta">{words.endMeta}</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <SiteFooter home="#top" docs={docs()} />
  </div>
</main>

